// One row per CHANGE to an Azure resource - what changed, when, by whom, and through which
// client. Fourteen days, which is all Resource Graph keeps.
//
// Loaded by Search-MsecAzureResourceGraph -ResourceType ResourceChange.
// Its companion, -Name Properties, gives one row per changed PROPERTY with the old and new
// values - that is the one that answers "who changed this specific setting".
//
// FOURTEEN DAYS IS A HARD CEILING. Resource Graph keeps change records for 14 days and there is
// no setting to extend it. Measured on a live tenant: oldest record exactly 14 days old. Asking
// for more returns nothing rather than failing, so an empty result for last month is NOT
// "nothing changed" - it is "out of retention". For anything longer-lived you need the Activity
// Log (90 days) or a diagnostic setting shipping it to a workspace.
//
// THIS IS A SNAPSHOT DIFF, NOT AN AUDIT LOG. Resource Graph periodically snapshots each resource
// and reports the difference between consecutive snapshots. The consequences worth knowing:
//   * Two changes between snapshots collapse into one record, and a change that was reverted
//     before the next snapshot leaves no trace at all.
//   * Only properties Resource Graph projects are diffed. That covers the resource body, which
//     is why a patch-orchestration change shows up (see below), but not sub-resources it does
//     not model.
//   * The Activity Log remains the authoritative record of who called what.
//
// MOST OF THE VOLUME IS THE PLATFORM TALKING TO ITSELF. ChangedByType 'System' is Azure updating
// its own runtime fields - provisioningState, instanceView, powerState - and on a live tenant
// that was 562 of the records with no human involved. Filter on ChangedByType to get to changes
// somebody made:
//     Search-MsecAzureResourceGraph -ResourceType ResourceChange |
//         Where-Object ChangedByType -in 'User', 'Application'
// It is NOT filtered here, because "the platform restarted this VM" is a real answer to "why did
// this change", and a query that hid it would make the platform's own actions invisible.
//
// PATCH ORCHESTRATION IS IN HERE. patchMode lives on the VM resource body
// (properties.osProfile.windowsConfiguration.patchSettings.patchMode), so moving a machine
// between 'Windows Automatic Updates' (AutomaticByOS) and an Azure-orchestrated mode is an ARM
// write on the VM and is diffed like any other property - whether it was done in the Azure
// Update Manager blade, the CLI or a template. Use -Name Properties to see it:
//     Search-MsecAzureResourceGraph -ResourceType ResourceChange -Name Properties |
//         Where-Object PropertyName -like '*patchSettings*'
//
// CHANGEDBY IS NOT ALWAYS A PERSON. 'User' carries a sign-in name, 'Application' a service
// principal, 'System' is Azure itself, and 'Unspecified' is a change whose origin was not
// recorded. All four are reported as they come rather than normalised, because "Azure did it"
// and "we do not know who did it" are different answers.
//
// NB: no time filter in this file. Resource Graph has no timespan parameter - filter in
// PowerShell on ChangedUtc, which is a real datetime rather than one of the strings the Log
// Analytics API hands back.

resourcechanges
| extend Attributes = properties.changeAttributes
| extend ResourceId = tostring(properties.targetResourceId)
| project ChangedUtc     = todatetime(Attributes.timestamp),
          ChangeType     = tostring(properties.changeType),
          // The resource name is only present inside the id.
          ResourceName   = tostring(split(ResourceId, '/')[-1]),
          ResourceType   = tostring(properties.targetResourceType),
          // 'User', 'Application', 'System' or 'Unspecified' - see the note above.
          ChangedByType  = tostring(Attributes.changedByType),
          ChangedBy      = tostring(Attributes.changedBy),
          // How it was done: Azure Portal, CLI, PowerShell, ARM Template, Terraform, .NET SDK,
          // a DevOps task, or a bare GUID for a first-party service with no friendly name.
          Client         = tostring(Attributes.clientType),
          // The ARM operation, e.g. Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines/write.
          Operation      = tostring(Attributes.operation),
          ChangeCount    = toint(Attributes.changesCount),
          // Every property that moved, named. -Name Properties gives the values.
          ChangedProperties = strcat_array(bag_keys(properties.changes), ', '),
          CorrelationId  = tostring(Attributes.correlationId),
          ResourceGroup  = tostring(resourceGroup),
          SubscriptionId = tostring(subscriptionId),
          ResourceId
| order by ChangedUtc desc
